\section{Treat Generation as a Join of Two Inputs}

A native header can tell a binding generator about names,
declarator structure, attributes, typedefs and certain
compile-time constants under the chosen preprocessor
configuration. It cannot reliably tell it how long a
buffer is kept, whether a factory returns an owned handle,
or whether a callback can run on a thread with no VM
attachment. Generate raw declarations from a target C
front end; join those declarations against a reviewed
policy manifest before generating an ordinary host API.
Missing policy for a pointer-bearing operation need not
prevent a raw binding from being generated, but it must
prevent a supposedly safe wrapper from being generated.

Take a signal library exporting an operation named
\texttt{series\_read} with a handle, mutable point buffer,
capacity, and written count. The header establishes that
the count uses \texttt{size\_t}. The policy says capacity
and count are points, not bytes, only successful status
initializes the count, and the point array is not retained.
A change from points to bytes could preserve the entire
binary signature and still require a binding change. In the
other direction, an ABI change such as a new record member
may preserve the conceptual operation but invalidate compiled
raw code. Version and review the two inputs separately.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lp{0.62\linewidth}}
\hline
Artifact & What it supplies or checks \\
\hline
Parsed target header & Type graph, symbol spellings, attributes, constants. \\
Policy manifest & Ownership and borrow rules, units, status refinements. \\
Native compile checks & Record offsets, alignment and compatible prototypes. \\
Cross-language fixtures & Observable call placement and runtime policies. \\
Release metadata & Header configuration and supported native revisions. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Verify With an Independent Authority}

Regenerating the raw declarations and comparing them with
an earlier generated file identifies drift; it does not
show which result is correct. Emit a C probe that includes
the real header rather than rebuilding the header from the
binding. For records whose layout the host consumes, have
the target compiler check \texttt{sizeof}, alignment and
offsets against numeric literals taken from the host view.
For functions, compare the compiler's concrete declaration
with a generated expected function-pointer type, using a
compile-time type check or an assignment with warnings made
fatal as appropriate to the toolchain. Do not introduce a
cast to silence a mismatch. For macros, compile a facade
that invokes the macro as the header intends. None of these
checks requires executing host-built probes while
cross-compiling.

Compile and run a separate native fixture on the actual
target as well. The System V supplement illustrates why:
a by-value record can have correct offsets but be passed
using a classification the raw binding did not implement.
The fixture should write and read distinct payloads under
separate compilation so accidental agreement between two
passes of the generator cannot hide a mismatch. The two
arms in \Cref{fig:visual-395} answer different questions:
what the wrapper claims, and which claims the target compiler
and running fixture corroborate.

