\section{Fail Generation at Unsupported or Stale Policy}

\begin{verbatim}
generate(target, header, manifest):
    index := parse_c(header, target.triple, target.flags)
    seen_policy := empty_set()
    for item in sorted(index.public_declarations):
        shape := normalize_native_type(item, target)
        if unsupported(shape):
            emit_rejection(item, reason(shape))
            continue
        emit_raw_declaration(item.symbol, shape)
        emit_independent_compile_checks(item, shape, header)
        if has_policy(manifest, item.stable_name):
            policy := get_policy(manifest, item.stable_name)
            require(policy.expected_shape == fingerprint(shape))
            require(policy_mentions_valid_parameters(policy, item))
            verify_cleanup_on_every_exit(policy)
            emit_checked_wrapper(item, shape, policy)
            seen_policy.insert(item.stable_name)
    require(seen_policy == manifest.requested_wrappers)
\end{verbatim}

The shape fingerprint covers calling convention, type identity
where relevant, and all layout and signature details used by
the output. It is not an ownership fingerprint. The manifest
must still be reviewed when a library release changes semantics
without changing the ABI. Deterministic sort order and a recorded
target configuration make a generated diff usable by reviewers.
A native library with a separately versioned ABI can expose a
small version query; resolve that trusted entry and check
compatibility before opening the rest of the surface.

\section{Track the Proof Boundary of Static Checking}

Furr and Foster \cite{furr2008ffi} demonstrate how multilingual analysis can
model C access to Java and OCaml values; for instance, a tag
check can refine which representation operations are permitted
on a subsequent branch. A binding generator can use analogous
local checks when compiling wrappers: a borrowed reference
cannot outlive its root frame, and an output field may be read
only on the branch that initializes it. Proving these properties
of generated glue is not a proof that an external library
obeys its documented retention or status rules.

For the signal library, force the supplier to report a short
successful read, write partial storage and fail, and report an
out-of-range successful count. The wrapper publishes the short
record sequence only for the first case. Run these alongside
tests that deliberately fail compilation when configured
packing or a typedef changes. Examine which check failed:
that makes it possible to identify a declaration mismatch,
a policy mismatch or an implementation violation instead
of treating all three as an opaque broken binding.

\section{Version Layout Facts Separately from Semantic Reviews}

Imagine two versions of the signal library. One adds a
timestamp to the point record at the end; the other leaves
the record unchanged but starts clearing output on a documented
short-read status. The first changes array stride and likely
the shape fingerprint. A native build of the old fixture
including the new header should fail its size or stride
assertion even though the leading point coordinates still
occupy the same offsets. The second version compiles under
the old declaration but needs a reviewed policy and updated
expectations about when to read or publish the result.

Keep separate verdicts in CI, for instance a target type
probe, a linked integration fixture and a release policy
review. Record the library identity used by each integration
run; a successful compile can exercise one header while
the loader finds a different binary of the same name. If
platform metadata and runtime version queries do not establish
compatibility, tie the installed library and binding together
as one tested distribution artifact. A version string does
not override explicit layout or calling-convention evidence.

Generation should be deterministic across machines configured
for the same target. Avoid ambient include paths and producer
timestamps in generated declarations. Normalize source locations
in the provenance record without changing the actual compiler
arguments. Preserve the original declaration location for
diagnostics so a rejected declaration is actionable. If
a policy entry becomes unused because a symbol disappeared
behind a preprocessor condition, make the generator fail
or mark that policy explicitly inapplicable to the target;
silently dropping it can remove a public operation unnoticed.

As a final negative check, change one manifest assertion to
say that the returned count is initialized on all statuses.
Use a fixture whose failure path leaves the count untouched,
and instrument the wrapper so that a read of the output
cell is detected. Compilation and native layout should still
pass. Only the policy test should fail, demonstrating why a
header-only pipeline cannot stand in for runtime verification.
